The '''Kingdom of Galwa (1743–1822)''' refers to the period of the [[Burawi Kingdom of Galwa|western Burawi kingdom]] from the [[National Reorganization Edict of 1743]] to the conclusion of the [[Burawi War]] and the creation of [[Kijura]] as a buffer state. The period encompasses the reigns of [[Cali IV]] (1743–c. 1780s) and [[Nuur VI]] (c. 1780s–1822), the colonization of [[Burawi North Kaftia|North Kaftia]], and the armed conflict with the eastern [[Kingdom of Burawa]] that ended the kingdom's independence.

Following the loss of the colonial protectorate of [[Bindorama]] and the creation of [[Asikyira]] in 1742, the Kingdom of Galwa pursued an economic and diplomatic reorientation. The regency and early reign of Cali IV had been characterised by consolidation and internal reform; the later reign saw the kingdom pivot toward external trade and colonial expansion. The [[Volisanian Civil War]] (1736–1801), which disrupted Illypnian colonial systems, created opportunities for Galwa to develop new commercial relationships, including a cotton trade agreement with the [[Zong|Kingdom of Zong]] and a military alliance with [[Breisland]]. Galwa also pursued colonization in North Kaftia, establishing ports and inland territories that brought the kingdom into direct competition with Burawa.

These tensions culminated in the [[Burawi War]] (1811–1822), an eleven-year conflict that exhausted both kingdoms and concluded with the creation of [[Kijura]] as a neutral buffer state separating the two Burawi polities.

== Reign of Cali IV (1743–c. 1780s) ==

{{main|Cali IV of Galwa}}

[[Cali IV]] assumed direct rule following the death of Regent [[Sahra of Galwa|Sahra]] in 1740. The early years of his reign were consumed by the conclusion of the [[Bindorama Civil War]] (1735–1742) and the creation of the [[Parliament of Lords (Galwa)|Parliament of Lords]] through the [[National Reorganization Edict of 1743]]. Having survived the colonial crisis, Cali IV turned to the economic and diplomatic challenges posed by the loss of Bindorama and the broader disruptions of the 18th century.

=== Economic reorientation ===

The loss of Bindorama deprived Galwa of its primary source of colonial goods, particularly cotton. The western kingdom had depended on Bindorama's plantation economy for textile production and export revenues; the creation of [[Asikyira]] in 1742 removed this supply from Galwa's control. Simultaneously, the eastern [[Kingdom of Burawa]] controlled access to [[Lacashe]], the largest cotton-producing colony in the Burawi sphere. Galwa's loss of Bindorama and exclusion from Lacashe created a supply crisis.

=== Volisanian Civil War and trade opportunities ===

{{main|Volisanian Civil War}}

The [[Volisanian Civil War]] (1736–1801) disrupted the colonial systems of the [[United Kingdoms of Volisania]], which had been the dominant Illypnian power in the [[Aethiopean Ocean]] and the [[Mesogean Sea|Mesogean]]. The war created a power vacuum that affected colonial possessions across [[Abyala]] and [[Kaftia]]. Volisanian naval protection declined; colonial shipping became vulnerable to piracy; and the metropolitan government lost the capacity to enforce trade monopolies.

Galwa, like other secondary powers, exploited the disruption. The kingdom courted former Volisanian colonies that had achieved de facto autonomy during the war, including the settlements that would later form the [[Hesperian League]] in Abyala and the [[Balisca]]n concessions. Galwa sought to establish itself as an alternative trading partner and security provider, offering naval protection and commercial access in exchange for preferential trade terms.

==== Galwa-Zong cotton trade ====

The most significant commercial agreement of Cali IV's reign was the [[Galwa-Zong trade agreement|trade agreement]] with the [[Zong|Kingdom of Zong]], the dominant maritime power of eastern [[Fosia]]. Zong possessed colonial possessions in the New World, including the territory of [[Sinchew]], which produced cotton for export.

The agreement, negotiated in the 1750s, established a direct trade route between Zong's Sinchew colony and Galwa's ports on the [[Aethiopean Ocean]]. Zong gained access to Kaftian markets and a reliable buyer for its colonial cotton; Galwa secured an alternative supply source independent of Burawi-controlled Lacashe. The cotton trade funded Galwa's textile industry and provided export revenues that partially compensated for the loss of Bindorama.

==== Galwa-Breisland alliance ====

{{main|Galwa-Breisland alliance}}

Galwa also pursued a military alliance with [[Breisland]], an Illypnian power that had established colonial footholds in northern Kaftia. Breisland, competing with Volisania and other Illypnian powers for colonial advantage, sought allies in the region. Galwa, seeking to balance against the eastern kingdom of Burawa, welcomed Breisland as a partner.

Under the terms of the alliance, Galwa permitted Breisland to dock military vessels at Galwa ports. In exchange, Breisland provided military supplies, naval expertise, and diplomatic support. The alliance gave Galwa access to modern military technology and training, while providing Breisland with a strategic foothold on the Aethiopean coast.

=== North Kaftian colonization ===

{{main|North Kaftia}}

Galwa's most ambitious initiative during Cali IV's reign was the colonization of [[Burawi North Kaftia|North Kaftia]], the coastal region north of the [[Great Kaftian Lake]]. The unified Burawi kingdom had established forts and trading posts in the region during the 17th century; these had been divided between the two successor states in 1709, with Burawa retaining most facilities. Galwa, seeking its own colonial foothold, initiated a program of settlement and expansion.

The colonization proceeded in stages. In the 1760s and 1770s, Galwa established ports along the North Kaftian coast, providing safe harbours for trade vessels and bases for commercial operations. In the 1780s, colonization expanded inland, with Galwa settlers establishing agricultural settlements and mines in the interior. The North Kaftian colonies provided Galwa with new sources of raw materials and reinforced the kingdom's position as a colonial power.

== Reign of Nuur VI (c. 1780s–1822) ==

{{main|Nuur VI of Galwa}}

Cali IV died in the 1780s and was succeeded by his son, [[Nuur VI]]. The transition was orderly; the Parliament of Lords, created by Cali IV, confirmed the succession and provided institutional continuity. Nuur VI continued his father's policies, deepening Galwa's engagement with North Kaftia and maintaining the alliances that had sustained the kingdom's economy.

=== North Kaftian expansion ===

Under Nuur VI, Galwa's North Kaftian colonization accelerated. The kingdom expanded its inland territories, establishing administrative structures and granting land to settlers and colonial companies. The expansion brought Galwa's colonial holdings into closer proximity with Burawa's own North Kaftian territories, creating territorial overlaps and disputes.

North Kaftia became a point of contention between the two Burawi kingdoms. Burawa viewed Galwa's expansion as encroachment on territory that had historically been within the Burawi sphere. Galwa, citing the 1709 settlement's ambiguity regarding North Kaftian boundaries, asserted its right to colonise. Local incidents between Galwa and Burawa settlers increased in frequency throughout the first decade of the 19th century.

The new colonization drive was increasingly financed through crown companies with merchant investors. The loss of Bindorama and the relative impoverishment of Galwa after Sahra's long regency encouraged the crown to treat overseas expansion as an expeditionary necessity rather than a purely commercial choice. During the same period, the state created a national bank and authorised the sale of royal land to wealthy commoners, measures intended to mobilise capital for colonization and reduce the crown's dependence on ecclesiastical wealth.

=== Growing tensions ===

The relationship between the crown and the [[Burawi Catholic Church]] became increasingly strained during this period. The kingdom's earlier dependence on ecclesiastical institutions for education and administration gave way to a more explicit doctrine of royal authority, or regalism, particularly under Nuur VI. Although the Church retained its headquarters at [[Weli Markos]] and remained influential at court, royal officials increasingly viewed ecclesiastical landholdings and clerical autonomy as obstacles to state consolidation.

That tension had practical consequences for the crown's finances. Galwa's expanded colonization and naval commitments required new sources of revenue, and the monarchy responded by selling royal land to wealthy commoners and encouraging the growth of crown companies with merchant investors. The sale of land and the creation of a national bank were intended to mobilise capital for state-building and reduce dependence on landed and ecclesiastical wealth.

== Burawi War (1811–1822) ==

{{main|Burawi War}}

The [[Burawi War]] was an armed conflict between the [[Kingdom of Burawa]] and the [[Burawi Kingdom of Galwa]] that lasted from 1811 to 1822. The war arose from a combination of territorial disputes, colonial competition, and long-term tensions between the two successor states of the 1709 split.

=== Causes ===

The immediate cause of the war was a border incident between ranchers in the disputed border region. A conflict over grazing lands escalated when local authorities intervened, each kingdom supporting its own subjects. The intervention escalated to military deployment, and the situation spiralled into open warfare.

The underlying causes included:
* Territorial disputes in North Kaftia, where both kingdoms claimed overlapping regions
* Long-term competition for colonial resources and trade
* Tensions arising from Galwa's alliance with Breisland, seen by Burawa as an Illypnian encroachment
* Religious and diplomatic tensions, as the Church's intermediary role failed to contain the conflict

=== Course of the war ===

The war lasted eleven years, from 1811 to 1822. Both kingdoms mobilised their armies and navies, with Burawa drawing upon its North Kaftian and Lacashe resources and Galwa relying upon its Breisland alliance and Zong trade connections.

The conflict was fought along the disputed border between the two kingdoms and in North Kaftia, where colonial forces clashed. The war proved indecisive; neither kingdom could achieve a decisive victory. The mountainous terrain of the border region favoured defensive operations, and both sides suffered heavy casualties in repeated offensives that gained little ground.

=== Creation of Kijura ===

{{main|Kijura}}

By 1822, both kingdoms were exhausted. The war had depleted treasuries, devastated border regions, and produced no decisive outcome. Peace negotiations, mediated by the [[Cardinal of Burawa]] from neutral [[Weli Markos]], produced a settlement.

Under the terms of the [[Treaty of Jawi|peace settlement]], the contested border region was separated from both kingdoms and constituted as an independent buffer state. The new state, named [[Kijura]] (Burawi for "the middle place"), was placed under the rule of [[Lord Cusmaan]], a Galwa noble of distinguished lineage but limited political prospects in Galwa itself. The arrangement removed a potential challenger from Galwa while providing Burawa with a buffer against its western rival.

Kijura was, however, created for political rather than ethnic purposes; the Kijuran people residing in the territory were placed under a newly constituted government that they had not sought.

== Legacy ==

The period from 1743 to 1822 established Galwa as a regional power with colonial possessions and international alliances. The economic and diplomatic reorientation pursued by Cali IV and Nuur VI compensated for the loss of Bindorama and provided the kingdom with new sources of wealth and influence.

The Burawi War, however, revealed the limits of Galwa's capacity. The eleven-year conflict exhausted the kingdom and concluded with the loss of the border territory that became Kijura. The war demonstrated that neither Burawi kingdom could achieve dominance over the other; the 1709 split had created a stable equilibrium that could be disturbed but not overturned.

The creation of Kijura marked the end of Galwa's expansionist phase. The kingdom would continue as an independent state for another century, but its strategic position had been fixed by the war and its consequences.

